{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 24, :comments ["<b>If you lend money to My people, to the poor man with you.</b> There appears to be a redundancy here, for he should have said, 'If you lend money to the poor man.' And first he called him 'My people,' and afterward 'poor,' and afterward 'your fellow,' for he said, 'If you take your fellow's garment as a pledge.' Rather, since he wanted to command him concerning two matters, therefore he prefaced for him two introductions — for from both sides you are obligated to lend to him. The first is from the side that he is included in 'My people,' and the way of the king is that at a time when his people and servants lack provisions, any person may lend to them on the assurance that the king will repay his debt, for it is incumbent upon the king to sustain his people, and one who gives to them is as if he gives to the king himself, and therefore he is assured of repayment of the debt. So too this poor man sits within 'My people,' and upon HaShem he casts his hope, and 'he who is gracious to the poor lends to YHWH' (Proverbs 19:17), for one who is gracious to the poor lends to HaShem and not to him. The second is that the poor man does more with you than you do with him, as is concluded (Bava Batra 10a): it was taught, Rabbi Meir would say… and it concludes there: if your Elohim desires the poor, why does He not provide for them? You too say to Him: in order that we may be saved from the judgment of Gehinnom. And if so, you do with him the life of this hour and he does with you the life of the world to come — you give him a coin and the Holy One, blessed be He, repays you double, twofold. Therefore it is said (Ruth 2:19), 'that I have done with him today' — and our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Vayikra Rabbah 34:8), 'The poor man does more with the householder than the householder does with the poor man.' Therefore it says here 'to the poor man with you,' and the expression 'with you' is all-encompassing — for because he is with you, therefore he has come to poverty, in order to merit you." "And corresponding to these two, He commanded him concerning two matters. Corresponding to what He said, 'If you lend money to My people' (Exodus 22:24), He said, 'you shall not be to him like a creditor' — as if He said, you shall not be like a creditor to him, for it is not to him that you are a creditor but to the Holy One, blessed be He, for one who is gracious to the poor is called 'one who lends to YHWH' (Proverbs 19:17) and not to him, and the Master of your work is trustworthy to repay you on behalf of His people. Therefore you shall not press him to demand repayment with force, as Rashi explained, for why should you press him when it was not to him that you lent, but rather to the King, blessed be He. And corresponding to what He said, 'the poor man with you' (Exodus 22:24) — this poor man who does more with you than you do with him — concerning this He said, 'you shall not lay interest upon him' (Exodus 22:24), so as to profit from your money. Do you belittle in your eyes what the poor man does with you, this much and that much — whether from the side that your reward is very great and you yet seek further profit from him, or from the side that this poor man became poor for your sake, for in order to save you from the judgment of Gehinnom the Holy One, blessed be He, does not provide for him Himself, as explained above. And if despite all this the poverty and distress he suffers with you on your behalf in order to merit you — and how can it enter your mind to seek further profit from him? This is nothing but evil of heart." "And what He said — 'you shall lay' (תְשִׂימוּן) in the plural — is to warn all of them: the lender and the borrower and the guarantor and the witnesses — all of them transgress by 'you shall not lay.' And if you stumbled by being lacking in faith and not believing that it shall be repaid to you from the hidden heavenly treasury stored away for one who does righteousness upon the earth, then this is your punishment in the end: 'you shall take your fellow's garment as a pledge' (Exodus 22:25), for you too will come to poverty and you will be the fellow of the poor man, his companion in poverty, and you will be compelled to take a pledge from him, since you will be poor like him. And this is the meaning of the doubling of 'take … you shall take' (חָבֹל תַּחְבֹּל). Nevertheless I warn you that 'until the sun comes you shall return it to him' (Exodus 22:25), for if you deviated from the path of uprightness and already took a pledge from him, then see to it, correspondingly, that you return the pledge to him before sunset. And it appears that he is speaking of a garment that covers by night, for it says, 'in what he sleeps' (Exodus 22:26)." "And by way of allusion He mentioned the sun, to say: look upon the sun that I created to serve you by way of kindness and righteousness, for the Holy One, blessed be He, renews in His goodness every day, always, the work of Creation, and all the commandments that are performed in this world — those who fear YHWH and contemplate His Name — none of them are worthy of this great kindness that the Holy One, blessed be He, makes the sun rise for them every day, as it is written (Malachi 3:20), 'and for you who fear My name, a sun of righteousness shall shine.' For even upon those who fear YHWH, the sun shines by way of righteousness; and if so, Solomon spoke well (Ecclesiastes 2:11), 'and there is no advantage under the sun' — for in all the deeds of man, there is not in them enough to repay the Holy One, blessed be He, for this great kindness. And therefore He said, 'until the sun comes you shall return it to him' (Exodus 22:25), for you shall contemplate the sun of righteousness that I do with you, and then you shall take instruction and from Me you shall see, and so shall you do — just as I make the sun shine for you, so shall you shine for this poor man who walks in darkness and has no brightness, as it is written (Isaiah 58:7–8), 'Is it not to share your bread with the hungry…,' and adjacent to it, 'then your light shall break forth as the dawn…,' 'and your light shall shine in the darkness…'" "And there is yet another allusion in His saying, 'until the sun comes you shall return it to him' — to the rich among the Hebrews who hold pledges of the poor, and who instruct their heirs to return them to the poor after their death, that is, after the setting of their sun, as it is written (Ecclesiastes 12:2), 'before it grows dark….' Therefore it is said, 'until the sun comes you shall return it to him.' For what fool would turn aside from here so as not to fulfill the commandment during his lifetime, when he is commanded in it, and instead perform the commandment after his death, when he is no longer commanded in it — for it is said (Psalms 88:6), 'among the dead, free' — once a person dies, he becomes free of the commandments. And those who do so without doubt have no reward for their toil, since he did not fulfill the commandment in its time when he was obligated in it, and he wishes to fulfill it after his death when he sees that then he cannot use his money at all. And what advantage is there to one who does righteousness at that time? Whether from the side that then his money is not in his possession, or from the side that he no longer has need of it, or from the side that he is free of the commandments — yet in his lifetime his hands refused to do, and in his death he wishes to be righteous." "And by way of allusion we may explain, the verse too — ״כִּי הִוא כְסוּתוֹ לְבַדָּהּ הִיא שִׂמְלָתוֹ לְעֹרוֹ בַּמֶּה יִשְׁכָּב״ — as referring to the man whose eye is evil toward the poor and who does not wish to give charity in his lifetime before the sun comes; if so, with what shall he lie down in the grave? For ״הִוא״ — that is, the charity — is ״כְּסוּתוֹ לְבַדָּהּ,״ his only covering, for when he dies he is buried naked of this commandment and has no other covering except charity, as it says (Job 29:14) ״צֶדֶק לָבַשְׁתִּי וַיִּלְבָּשֵׁנִי״. And our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Bava Batra 9a) that charity is likened to a garment, for every thread and thread joins together into a great garment — so too charity: every coin and coin joins together into a great reckoning. Therefore it says ״בַּמֶּה יִשְׁכָּב,״ and therefore ״כְּסוּתֹהּ״ is written with a ה׳, to expound from it that it is the covering of the soul of the lender." "And we may say further that this entire verse is according to its plain sense, and in any case it sets down these details so that the giver — or the one who does not give — may learn from them: whatever he does to the poor man is done also to him. For the warning ״עַד בֹּא הַשֶּׁמֶשׁ תְּשִׁיבֶנּוּ לוֹ״ carries in its meaning both an unspecified repayment in this world and a repayment to the poor, until the coming of his sunset — the time of departure and freedom, which is before the time of death as mentioned. And afterward it says that if he does not give to the poor before the sun comes, then the poor man has nothing with which to lie down; likewise he himself will have nothing with which to lie down in the grave, for charity alone is his covering and his garment for his skin, and apart from it he has no other garment, and he lies naked in the valley of the shadow of death. As he did to this poor man in the night, so shall be done also to him. And this is a correct and exceedingly precious allusion — all the more so for those cliques who do not instruct even their heirs to return, and behold: the heirs take their wealth while they themselves bear their iniquity. Woe for that disgrace and shame, for I have seen in our generation many rich men who sit in the dust, for they stumble in this and in similar things, and they take no rebuke in their lifetimes. And narrowness of eye has become a possession clinging to them in their lives, and in their death they are not separated — and enough with this small reminder."]